{%- comment %}
Wraps an external target link in @zachleat/browser-window's browser chrome —
the last pinned-but-unreferenced dependency.

The source uses this for in-content link previews (its `browserWindow`
shortcode, eleventy.config.js:455-469), where it also runs colour extraction
off a screenshot service to tint the chrome. That half is deliberately not
ported: it needs a screenshot service and network access at build time, and
this theme's builds are offline.

What carries over is the affordance itself. A bookmark and a reply are both
"this post is about that URL", and browser chrome around the link says that
more clearly than a bare anchor.

Progressive enhancement, following the source's own island pattern: without
JS, <browser-window> is an unknown element and renders its children as-is —
a plain link. Callers pass `linkClass` so the microformats property
(u-bookmark-of / u-in-reply-to) stays on the anchor.
{% endcomment -%}
{%- comment %} h-cite. The mf2 spec allows u-in-reply-to / u-like-of /
   u-repost-of / u-bookmark-of to take EITHER a URL or an embedded h-cite
   (microformats.org/wiki/h-entry), and the embedded form is what lets a
   consumer show who and what is being responded to rather than a bare link.
   So the property moves to the container and the anchor carries u-url + p-name,
   which are h-cite's own properties.

   Indiekit stores these as plain URL strings, so the URL-only shape is what a
   real site produces today and it must read correctly on its own. `linkTarget`
   is therefore accepted as either a string or an object: a Micropub client that
   sends richer reply context (JF2 permits a nested object) gets p-author and
   dt-published for free, and nothing is fabricated when it doesn't. Fetching
   the target to fill those in would mean network at build time, which this
   theme's builds do not do. {% endcomment -%}
{%- assign citeUrl = linkTarget.url | default: linkTarget -%}
{%- comment %} p-name only when a REAL name exists. Falling back to the URL
   would assert that a URL is the citation's title — the same falsehood removed
   from post p-name for the four titleless types. With no name, the anchor
   carries u-url alone and a parser implies the name from the link text, which
   is what h-cite expects. {% endcomment -%}
{%- assign citeName = linkTarget.name | default: linkText -%}
{%- comment %} …and never when the "name" IS the url. An untitled bookmark's
   title falls back to its own bookmarkOf url (_data/eleventyComputed.js), so
   a naive `linkText: title` would hand this the url and claim it as a title.
   Compare rather than trust the caller: this holds for any caller, now or
   later. {% endcomment -%}
{%- if citeName and citeName != citeUrl %}{% assign citeClass = "u-url p-name" %}{% else %}{% assign citeClass = "u-url" %}{% assign citeName = citeName | default: citeUrl %}{% endif -%}
<div class="{{ linkClass }} h-cite">
<is-land on:idle on:visible>
{%- if JS_ENABLED %}<template data-island><script type="module" src="/browser-window.js"></script></template>{% endif %}
<browser-window url="{{ citeUrl }}" icon shadow flush>
  <a class="{{ citeClass }}" href="{{ citeUrl }}">{{ citeName }}</a>
</browser-window>
</is-land>
{%- if linkTarget.author or linkTarget.published %}
<p class="cite-meta">
{%- if linkTarget.author %}<span class="p-author h-card">{% if linkTarget.author.url %}<a class="p-name u-url" href="{{ linkTarget.author.url }}">{{ linkTarget.author.name | default: linkTarget.author.url }}</a>{% else %}<span class="p-name">{{ linkTarget.author.name | default: linkTarget.author }}</span>{% endif %}</span>{% endif -%}
{%- if linkTarget.author and linkTarget.published %} · {% endif -%}
{%- if linkTarget.published %}<time class="dt-published" datetime="{{ linkTarget.published | isoInstant }}">{{ linkTarget.published | humanDate }}</time>{% endif -%}
</p>
{%- endif %}
</div>